WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.769 --> 00:00:03.352
(upbeat music)

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:07.500
- Hello, and welcome to
the South American segment

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:11.330
of the Peabody Museums,
a Fiesta Latina survey

00:00:11.330 --> 00:00:14.223
of the national birds of
Latin-American countries.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:20.203
Let's maybe begin with this big bird here.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:25.590
This actually is an
immature Andean condor,

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:29.950
condor andino, huge bird,

00:00:29.950 --> 00:00:32.700
the size of the California condor

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.070
with a three-meter wingspan.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:37.270
And this is a baby because

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:39.780
it takes them eight years

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:40.980
to achieve their adulthood.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:45.250
and the specimens we
have here are immature.

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:47.740
This bird amazingly was selected

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:50.760
as a national bird for
four different countries

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:51.820
in South America,

00:00:51.820 --> 00:00:55.480
Colombia, Ecuador, Bolivia, and Chile.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:56.720
And this is the bird

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:59.620
that to everybody symbolizes

00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:04.620
the Andes and it majestic glider

00:01:04.747 --> 00:01:06.460
and relies on Carrion.

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:09.380
So it spends most of the time searching

00:01:10.393 --> 00:01:12.520
for that guanaco some other Llama

00:01:14.059 --> 00:01:16.150
or they can sometimes
descend to the coast.

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:20.100
And in Chile are known to eat things

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:24.200
like beached whales or birthing seals.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:28.400
And this bird can reach about 15 kilograms

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:29.320
in body weight.

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:31.380
And in this case females

00:01:31.380 --> 00:01:33.093
are slightly smaller than males.

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:38.270
We have here

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:41.523
a very bizarre looking bird named hoatzin.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.640
This is a national bird of Guyana where

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:49.620
it's called Canji Pheasant because

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:52.910
it looked to some early explorers

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:54.680
like a chicken-like bird.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:56.380
But in fact, it's a bird

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:59.490
with a distinct lineage

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:01.980
of really not very clear relationships.

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.160
There is not really
anything closely related

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:05.840
to it surviving today.

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:07.110
It's a South American bird

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:10.029
that lives in swamps

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:12.160
and along rivers

00:02:12.160 --> 00:02:16.740
and has two bizarre features.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:18.840
One is that the chicks

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:19.810
after they leave the nest,

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:21.470
they can climb around

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:25.200
and they have three
fingers in their wings.

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.230
And later these disappear.

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:31.660
Okay so here we have hoatzin adult.

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:34.470
And in this case the three fingers

00:02:34.470 --> 00:02:36.030
of the babies are no longer here.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:37.090
They get re-absorbed

00:02:38.484 --> 00:02:40.230
and the wing bones fuse.

00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:43.690
And the other feature that hoatzin

00:02:43.690 --> 00:02:44.590
is famous for

00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:46.930
is the fact that they eat leaves

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:50.070
and they have a foregut with microbes

00:02:50.070 --> 00:02:53.550
that help them digest
the plants, the leaves

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:55.570
and they spent a lot

00:02:55.570 --> 00:02:58.430
of time digesting like cows.

00:02:58.430 --> 00:02:59.740
This is kind of an equivalent

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:02.110
to what ruminants do.

00:03:02.110 --> 00:03:04.700
And now let's look

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:07.360
at the national bird of Peru.

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:10.610
This is the Gallito de las Rocas

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:12.670
or the cock of the rock.

00:03:12.670 --> 00:03:14.540
Here we have a male.

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:15.373
This is a bird

00:03:15.373 --> 00:03:18.020
that is still pretty common in most

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:19.860
of the Andean countries,

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:22.660
not only Peru in the cloud forest.

00:03:22.660 --> 00:03:24.980
And you can actually arrange a visit

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:26.746
to one of the lakes.

00:03:26.746 --> 00:03:29.080
These are the places where they display.

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:32.180
You can see that this crest here

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:35.720
is kind of flattened on the sides.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:37.010
Two layers of feathers,

00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:39.020
folding over the bill.

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:40.840
It's only found in males

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:43.040
females are drabber brown

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:46.780
and the name Gallito de
las Rocas maybe refers

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:51.780
to the fact that they
nest in a little caves

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:56.523
and attach their mud
nests to a rocky wall.

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:59.650
So then let's look

00:03:59.650 --> 00:04:04.640
at the bird chosen by
the country of Suriname.

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:08.440
This is in English, Great Kiskadee,

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:10.360
but in most countries the bird gets

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:14.760
the name onomatopoeic related to its song.

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:17.380
It's a type of tyrant flycatcher

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:21.250
and the song to the English speaker sounds

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:22.490
like kis-ka-dee.

00:04:22.490 --> 00:04:24.830
But to a person in Suriname,

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:26.820
it's (foreign language)

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:27.840
or (foreign language)

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:30.483
if you are a Dutch inhabitant of Suriname.

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:35.380
They also occur in the United States

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:37.150
in southern most Texas

00:04:37.150 --> 00:04:39.480
and they go all the way

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:40.600
to Northern Argentina.

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:42.980
So it's a pretty widely distributed bird

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:44.320
but very charismatic.

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:49.110
Found in cities and can easily

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:51.413
be seen throughout South America.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:53.300
So now let's look

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:55.942
at the national bird of Brazil.

00:04:55.942 --> 00:04:57.570
Sabiá-laranjeira as it's called

00:04:57.570 --> 00:04:59.660
in Portuguese and in English

00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:03.840
Rufous-bellied thrush, or robin,

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.470
same as the North American robin.

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:08.080
These are birds common

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:11.110
throughout sub-urban,

00:05:11.110 --> 00:05:13.218
rural, different habitats

00:05:13.218 --> 00:05:14.800
that are altered by humans

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:17.170
and it's one of the most common birds

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:21.040
in lower southern cone of South America

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:23.580
in Uruguay, Paraguay, Argentina

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:26.070
and parts of south-eastern Brazil.

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:28.520
This bird maybe looks not

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:29.800
the most colorful

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:31.550
but has a fantastic song.

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:34.578
And this is same with

00:05:34.578 --> 00:05:37.600
the Costa Rican equivalent,

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:40.370
was chosen for the song

00:05:40.370 --> 00:05:42.110
and for its commonness

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:45.593
in human-inhabited habitats.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:51.170
Then this colorful bird here is

00:05:51.170 --> 00:05:52.880
to a North American birdwatcher,

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:54.240
it's an oriole,

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:55.220
but this particular one

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:56.420
is called troupial

00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:59.740
And in Venezuela where
it's a national bird,

00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:02.013
they call it Turpial Venezolano

00:06:02.013 --> 00:06:03.620
or (foreign language)

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:05.230
because it's found in

00:06:06.310 --> 00:06:09.230
region of Guajira, among others

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:11.700
It's also found in the adjacent Colombia

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:14.423
and it's one of the
largest species of orioles.

00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:17.207
And it's a bizarre species

00:06:17.207 --> 00:06:18.980
and the reason for that is

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:21.546
that it doesn't build a typical hanging,

00:06:21.546 --> 00:06:23.060
hammock-type nest

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:25.210
but it actually reserves,

00:06:25.210 --> 00:06:27.160
takes over nests of other species.

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:28.920
It has to be a covered nest.

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:31.550
So it could be another oriole or cacique,

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:33.570
or even could be a stick nest

00:06:33.570 --> 00:06:34.900
of some funarid.

00:06:37.790 --> 00:06:39.160
Then let's look

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:41.620
at the bird chosen by Paraguay

00:06:41.620 --> 00:06:43.290
as a national bird.

00:06:43.290 --> 00:06:46.480
This is actually, here's the right species

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:49.120
but I only have an immature male.

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:51.580
So this one here, you can see

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:53.330
that still has a lot of,

00:06:53.330 --> 00:06:55.940
kind of greenish, gray feathers

00:06:55.940 --> 00:06:58.390
but already developing it's bare throat.

00:06:58.390 --> 00:07:00.460
In English it's called

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:02.450
bare-throated bellbird

00:07:02.450 --> 00:07:04.370
and this bird here I'm holding is a

00:07:04.370 --> 00:07:06.080
white bellbird related species,

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:07.470
but you cannot just imagine

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:08.560
that this one would

00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:09.930
look like this, pure white

00:07:09.930 --> 00:07:12.560
and just have a brilliantly bluish,

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:14.770
green bare throat.

00:07:14.770 --> 00:07:18.440
And the name in Paraguay

00:07:18.440 --> 00:07:19.470
where it's a national bird,

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:23.320
it's called Guira Campana or Guira pong,

00:07:23.320 --> 00:07:25.583
pong is a onomatopoeic.

00:07:26.666 --> 00:07:28.590
The bird is most famous

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:29.610
for its loud song.

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:32.350
It's one of the loudest
bird vocalizations.

00:07:32.350 --> 00:07:35.080
And it does sound a bit like a pong

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:37.363
or like an anvil hit with a hammer,

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:40.510
very loud and usually
from the forest canopy.

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:43.200
Then let's look

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:45.763
at the national bird of Uruguay.

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:50.530
Tero tero, or it's called Southern lapwing

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:51.363
in English,

00:07:52.482 --> 00:07:55.060
it has a very thin little crest,

00:07:55.060 --> 00:07:57.410
has a very interesting,

00:07:57.410 --> 00:07:59.350
spurs in the wings.

00:07:59.350 --> 00:08:01.850
You can maybe just barely see

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:03.570
a little orange spur.

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:05.840
Let me see, Oh, here we go.

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:07.150
And these could be used

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:08.450
in nest defense

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:11.020
when they chase away a fox

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:14.050
and the bird is very charismatic, loud,

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:16.780
attacks an intruder to his territory

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:18.110
especially when the chicks

00:08:18.110 --> 00:08:20.373
or eggs are around.

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:24.520
So in adjacent Brazil

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:27.560
it's also onomatopoeically
called kero kero.

00:08:27.560 --> 00:08:29.820
And so tero tero, kero kero

00:08:29.820 --> 00:08:32.320
and the species now expands

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:33.880
and goes pretty far north

00:08:33.880 --> 00:08:35.690
reaching Central America.

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:37.050
And it takes advantage actually

00:08:37.050 --> 00:08:38.950
from human disturbance

00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:41.170
and clearing of the forest.

00:08:41.170 --> 00:08:42.820
Now we're switching.

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:44.050
Here's the last example.

00:08:44.050 --> 00:08:46.390
And this one is a national bird

00:08:46.390 --> 00:08:48.200
of Argentina.

00:08:48.200 --> 00:08:52.170
This brown bird is a
called either hornero,

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:55.970
or Alonsito or Caserita.

00:08:55.970 --> 00:08:58.021
And the fame for this bird

00:08:58.021 --> 00:08:59.490
is their nest.

00:08:59.490 --> 00:09:01.590
So here, if you can maybe zoom

00:09:01.590 --> 00:09:04.920
in on this fortress of mud.

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:07.300
These nests are part of the landscape

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:09.730
and a pair of these in a sense

00:09:09.730 --> 00:09:11.920
that they are found on buildings,

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:13.630
on telephone poles.

00:09:13.630 --> 00:09:17.010
It's a really a common sight in Argentina,

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:19.383
in Brazil, Paraguay, Uruguay.

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:22.040
The nest is made by two

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:23.690
of these birds, by a pair,

00:09:23.690 --> 00:09:26.130
and somebody calculated

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:28.250
to use like 3000 big fulls of mud.

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:29.920
And this is mud mixed

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:30.753
with plant material.

00:09:30.753 --> 00:09:33.210
So it's like a dobi, very durable

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:34.700
and only members

00:09:34.700 --> 00:09:36.720
of this one group of birds,

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:38.310
genus furnarius build

00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:41.673
these big five-kilogram fortresses.

00:09:42.518 --> 00:09:45.101
(upbeat music)

